00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:05.269
amputation and the severity of that sort of
injury me is that, um, I had a lot of surgeries

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:06.269
to look forward to.

00:01:06.269 --> 00:01:09.210
I was going to be hospitalized for weeks or
months.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.740
And so the result of that is that I was on
a lot of pain medication, uh, for quite a

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.500
long time and thanks to the tolerance that
opioids built up.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.420
Um, I was on escalating doses of pain medication
for about two months total.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.570
And before that in your life, had you been
on opiate painkillers?

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:27.630
Not that I can recall.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.710
You know, maybe at some point when I was younger
I had a nasty broken bone from sports and

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:36.820
got Vicodin and probably wasn't even given
it by my parents.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:42.749
Um, I was considered opiate naive by the physician,
so I'd certainly not been on the pills for

00:01:42.749 --> 00:01:46.649
three or six months prior and maybe had never
been.

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:52.760
And when you were put on the drugs, was it
one particular drug the entire time or were

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.079
they, were there different ones?

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:56.210
Lots of different ones.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:01.200
Uh, so for this sort of accident I was a trauma
case, you know, you tend to get passed through

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.039
systems and so on the ambulance I'd get intravenous
morphine.

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:09.060
Um, when it got to the hospital they switched
that to fentanyl.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:16.280
Um, eventually, uh, my IB drug of choice,
not mine, but at the hospitals, uh, was hydromorphone

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.280
brand named allotted.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:23.250
Um, but then to kind of build up something
that I could take home, cause IB medicine

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.260
is not something that you typically can take
home.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.340
I was put on oxycodone and I comes in both
the immediate release form.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.110
So just normal oxycodon that goes into your
system as quick as oral medicines can.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.050
And then extended release oxycodone, which
most people know and are the brand name Oxycontin.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:45.430
So at what point in this or is there a point
or how early, is there a discussion about

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.150
their risk with these drugs?

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:52.120
You need to careful, we need to be careful
or did that not come up until you realize

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.610
that there was a problem?

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.590
Yeah, it never came up.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:01.400
I mean this is probably the most shocking
feature of my entire story is, you know, I

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.620
had this two month period where I was on high
and escalating doses of opioids and I was

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.950
in three different world-class hospitals on
the eastern seaboard.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:16.520
I had, you know, 10, 12, maybe more clinicians
who signed their name to a prescription for

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.020
these medications.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:23.190
I was seen by dozens of doctors and nurses
coming through and not a single person ever

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.100
said anything to me about, um, the formation
of dependence, which means that, you know,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.550
I'll withdrawal if I'm taking, if the medication
is taken away.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.410
Nobody talked about the risk of addiction.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:39.800
Uh, the only cost of these medications that
was ever raised was constipation.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.290
Everybody was very worried about constipation.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.230
But, uh, that was it.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:49.620
And what about as you talk about as you develop
a tolerance, you're on escalating, you know,

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.760
amounts of the active ingredients.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.650
Even at that point there was not a discussion
about, okay, these doses are starting to get

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:56.900
quite high now.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.900
No, and as a matter of fact, the only real
advice I got about pain regarding medication

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.190
was you have to stay ahead of the pain.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:10.400
A couple times early on I got behind the pain,
they say, and it's just excruciating.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.930
You really feel like you're going to die.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:13.100
Sometimes you hope you're going to die.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.121
The pain is just all consuming.

00:04:14.121 --> 00:04:18.400
And so they say, look, don't risk getting
behind the pain.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.410
Make sure you take the medication before you're
in that situation.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:24.290
And so, you know, I thought I was being a
good soldier.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:28.530
I was following doctor's orders and every
four hours, you know, I popped the next oxycodone

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.220
pill every 12 hours I popped the next oxycontin
pill.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:37.090
And if I felt it wearing off even a little
bit, I'd call a prescribing doc, say, hey,

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:38.100
my tolerance is going up.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:42.260
And he'd say, okay, let's bump it from 15
to 20 milligrams or whatever.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:46.340
So at what point did you realize that you
were starting to have a problem?

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.370
I never did.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:52.510
Um, I went to a trauma surgeon, follow up
appointments and, uh, so he's a guy I hadn't

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:56.970
seen and, and weeks, uh, he, you know, initially
pulled the bones in my foot back together

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.540
and then passed me off to other teams to fix
the rest of the foot.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:06.030
And, uh, he just kind of going through his
checklist and sees the doses I'm on and gets

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:10.100
very stern, very concerned, and says, you
know, these doses are far too high.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.870
You're two months out, you've got to get off
the meds now.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.710
Um, but he didn't offer any advice.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:17.570
You know, it's kind of like, you've got to
get off, but that's not my problem.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.430
I haven't been controlling this train.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:25.910
Um, so he sent me to the plastic surgeon who
had been writing the most recent prescriptions.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.590
And what did, what happened at that point?

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:33.670
Well, the plastic surgeon was much less concerned
and he also, um, just clearly didn't know

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:34.889
what he was doing.

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:39.450
You know, he, he would admit that much later
to me, he would apologize, but, um, he just

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:41.230
hadn't dealt with dependence before.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.740
And so he was like, Oh yeah, um, you have
to taper the pills.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:50.180
Uh, so let's divide your daily dose into four,
drop a quarter each week, and you'll be off

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.070
the medication in a month.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:57.060
And did that, I mean, when you were told that
number one, did you understand like the importance

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.060
and urgency of it?

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:02.170
And number two was that was that practical
advice that you could actually follow?

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:05.760
So I think my partner Saudia, I think she
was skeptical.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:06.760
Yes.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.840
She's a research scientist by training and
she asked some follow up questions.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.870
I didn't know anything.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:15.240
You know, I was still fairly traumatized and
out of it.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.070
Um, so I was like, yeah, you know, this is
the plan.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.930
Um, but no, it's, it's terrible advice.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:24.940
Um, so what I would find out much, much later
turns out more than a year later in my own

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:30.640
research, is that you practically never should
start tapering a dependent patient at faster

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.850
than a 10% dose reduction per week.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:36.130
And then some patients will be able to go
faster if they haven't been on for very long,

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:40.250
and some patients will need to go much, much
slower because the withdrawal will be so severe.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.130
And in my case, the withdrawal was very severe.

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:47.120
And so as soon as I dropped that first dose,
these medications that were really short,

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.120
half life.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.310
So on the first day that you drop a significant
dose, if you're dependent, you'll feel withdrawal

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:54.710
and it's excruciating.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:07:01.760
Was there a point at which you did not need
the doses you were on for the amount of pain

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:08.139
you were experiencing, but started to recognize
that there were other reasons why you were

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:10.010
inclined to take the medication?

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:14.520
That's a really good and really hard question.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:19.200
You know, it kind of requires me to be a mind
reader of my distant past health.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.200
Yeah.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:23.310
I kind of feel like the answer must be yes.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:30.120
Um, I was so concerned to stay ahead of the
pain and I was scared of what I had experienced

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.710
in the past.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:36.780
And so I did a lot of clock watching and I
did a lot of, you know, early medicating.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.750
Um, I was not chasing euphoria.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:43.080
That was not something that happened to me,
at least not yet.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.479
And so I don't think I was, you know, kind
of as far down the path towards addictive

00:07:47.479 --> 00:07:51.229
behavior as um, as you can certainly get.

00:07:51.229 --> 00:07:55.680
But I was, I was almost certainly medicating
more than I should have been more than I needed

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:56.680
to.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.650
And so what I knew to only much, much later,
and what I know now is that very few people

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.970
should be on that high dose for that long.

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:08.490
Um, because the opioids are going to cause
profound dependence.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.110
That's just the way they work.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:16.229
Uh, and they tend to not work very well as
analgesics, as pain medication and the long

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:17.229
term.

00:08:17.229 --> 00:08:21.710
And so likely what I should have done is starting
in the first couple of weeks out of the hospital,

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:26.140
I should have been as aggressively as possible
titrating down the dose and putting up with

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:27.760
the most pain possible.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:29.940
Um, and I was just in no place to do that.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.650
No one, no one told me that.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:32.650
So

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:36.769
how bad, like what would you say was the peak
of, of the problem ultimately in terms of,

00:08:36.769 --> 00:08:41.800
of, of dependence and then what did actually
ultimately work to start reducing the doses

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:42.800
properly?

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:43.800
Yeah.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:44.800
Um,

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:50.920
so the sick joke of opioid tapering is that
it gets worse the farther along you get because

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:56.080
if you're dropping by a set reduction each
week or each two weeks, um, then the more

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.259
you drop the higher percentage dose reduction.

00:08:59.259 --> 00:09:00.779
That is kind of complicated.

00:09:00.779 --> 00:09:01.779
Right?

00:09:01.779 --> 00:09:02.779
But yeah, first week it was only a quarter.

00:09:02.779 --> 00:09:03.809
The second week it was a third.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:08.319
The third week it was half and then the fourth
week you drop all the rest.

00:09:08.319 --> 00:09:09.319
Right.

00:09:09.319 --> 00:09:14.430
And so the severity of withdrawal is relative
to that percentage dose reduction.

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:20.120
And so every week got workers and it was in
the fourth week, um, that, you know, I hadn't

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:26.110
slept well and weeks and I hadn't slept at
all in days because withdrawal is the opposite

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:27.600
of the drug's effects.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:33.839
And so opioids to date you withdrawal keeps
you kind of jittery and hyper alert and say,

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:36.730
I felt like I had to kind of fire inside me.

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:41.350
And I was incredibly depressed because the
opposite of euphoria which you get with opioids

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:42.350
is dysphoria.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:46.529
And I cried all the time and I thought I was
profoundly broken.

00:09:46.529 --> 00:09:51.630
And in that fourth week I was, I was convinced
from the inside that I was going to die from

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:55.129
withdrawal and you could not have convinced
me otherwise.

00:09:55.129 --> 00:09:59.740
I thought that I had special access to just
help broken.

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:03.730
I was and I was pretty sure I wasn't going
to survive it.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:08.949
And once you got two and pass that fourth
week, what happened?

00:10:08.949 --> 00:10:09.949
Well,

00:10:09.949 --> 00:10:14.179
so basically, you know, the second part of
your earlier question was what worked and,

00:10:14.179 --> 00:10:15.410
and nothing worked.

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:21.709
I just, um, I was incredibly fortunate that
I had a really strong support system, namely

00:10:21.709 --> 00:10:22.709
my partner Saudia.

00:10:22.709 --> 00:10:25.749
I had a one and a half year old daughter at
home seen CNM.

00:10:25.749 --> 00:10:32.279
Um, and I tell a story about her in the book
where she kind of unwittingly probably she's

00:10:32.279 --> 00:10:36.149
one and a half years old, but she comforts
me in a particular moment where, where I'm

00:10:36.149 --> 00:10:40.999
at this cost, where I really think I'm not
going to survive this and what, what haunts

00:10:40.999 --> 00:10:46.220
me for the month after I finally make it out
of this is I am quite sure that I would not

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:51.410
have made it out if I hadn't so desperately
wanted to be a good husband again to be a

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.740
good father to get this life back.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:54.740
Right.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:59.050
And so what happens to everybody else who
doesn't have that support system, who doesn't

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:02.860
have a bunch of things in their life that
they're desperately looking forward to?

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:07.429
It would've been so easy to medicate away
the suffering, especially because doctors

00:11:07.429 --> 00:11:09.230
were telling me it was okay to do that.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.300
They were like, oh, we can't offer you any
advice.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.160
Just go back on the pills if it's that bad.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.160
Yeah.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.899
I think this is what's often missing from
the understanding of a in people who have

00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:23.250
not experienced this personally, which is,
it's very easy to just say, I don't know,

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:27.180
it just sounds like I could taper and then
switch to a bunch of Advil and then taper

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:32.529
the Advil and it just, it's hard to imagine
when you hear the stories that don't go the

00:11:32.529 --> 00:11:33.529
way yours went.

00:11:33.529 --> 00:11:37.329
But rather it goes to, you know, by, I couldn't
get the prescriptions.

00:11:37.329 --> 00:11:39.149
Buying the pills on the street was too expensive.

00:11:39.149 --> 00:11:40.889
So I ended up with heroin.

00:11:40.889 --> 00:11:45.019
It seems inconceivable to people who have
not actually ever had this medication.

00:11:45.019 --> 00:11:51.499
But the line between your path and that path
seems to be relatively a, a small margin is

00:11:51.499 --> 00:11:53.240
what I'm hearing from you.

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:54.800
It's razor thin.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.230
You're so, so right.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:00.499
Like that's the central insight here.

00:12:00.499 --> 00:12:03.439
And whenever anybody says to me, you know,
you are so strong, congratulations.

00:12:03.439 --> 00:12:05.259
I'm like coasting strong.

00:12:05.259 --> 00:12:07.660
I was, I was a complete nerd.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.459
I thought I was gonna die at every moment.

00:12:09.459 --> 00:12:14.110
I was dragged across the finish line by my
family.

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:17.060
And by the way, at the end of that story,
I did give up.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:19.839
I refilled a prescription because I couldn't
take it anymore.

00:12:19.839 --> 00:12:25.819
And just by luck, that was the night I fell
asleep without taking any more pills.

00:12:25.819 --> 00:12:28.470
And that, that sleep kind of pulled me back
together.

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:32.079
And after I thought I can finish detoxing,
but I was ready to go back.

00:12:32.079 --> 00:12:38.199
And what I had said to Saudia is if I go back
on the meds, I will never come off them because

00:12:38.199 --> 00:12:40.949
I will never be able to do this to myself
again.

00:12:40.949 --> 00:12:45.279
And we both knew that we knew that that was
my commitment and that was my belief and we

00:12:45.279 --> 00:12:47.000
decided to go back on them anyway.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.249
So yeah, like it sounds crazy.

00:12:48.249 --> 00:12:50.619
I'm a faculty member at Johns Hopkins.

00:12:50.619 --> 00:12:55.750
Um, I have all of this kind of access and
privilege that not everybody has.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:13:00.120
And I can tell you right now, if I had gone
back on the pills, which I was so close to

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:05.160
doing, and then I'd been cut off, I would
have been willing to buy them on the street.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:06.550
Boy,

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:10.399
it's a, it's, it's, that's a, that's an incredible
thing that I think a lot of people just don't

00:13:10.399 --> 00:13:12.121
understand until they're in it.

00:13:12.121 --> 00:13:13.739
And that's why this is so pernicious.

00:13:13.739 --> 00:13:17.819
And, and I, I don't know if you think it's
gotten, this was 2015 I don't know how much

00:13:17.819 --> 00:13:22.559
you know about whether protocols have improved
since then, but this clearly is still a major

00:13:22.559 --> 00:13:23.559
problem.

00:13:23.559 --> 00:13:24.559
It's a huge problem.

00:13:24.559 --> 00:13:30.759
Uh, I work with a lot of hospitals and healthcare
systems now and I, I speak at conferences

00:13:30.759 --> 00:13:35.300
and advise organizations, and what I can say
is that the best ones are at least asking

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.430
for this help.

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:38.870
And part of why I wrote the book, you know,
I want everyone to read it.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:40.869
I'm thrilled that you read it.

